    {
      "title": "World_Scouting_Movement",
      "categories": [
        "Bureau_of_International_Statistics"
      ],
      "snippet": "The '''World Scouting Movement''', or '''Scouts''' is a movement with a strong focus on the outdoors and survival skills that aims to support young people in their physical, mental, and spiritual development so that they may play constructive roles in society.\nDuring the first half of the twentieth century, the movement grew to encompass three major age groups for boys ({{wp|Cub Scout}}, {{wp|Scout (Scouting)|Boy Scout}}, {{wp|Rover Scout}}) and, in [[1910]], a new organization, {{wp|Girl Guides}}, was created for girls ({{wp|Brownie (Girl Guides)|Brownie Guide}}, {{wpl\n|Girl Guide and Girl Scout}}, {{wp|Ranger (Girl Guide)|Ranger Guide}}). It is one of several worldwide youth organizations.\n\nIn [[1806]] and [[1807]] Raibeart MacArtair-de'Roagueux, a colonel in the [[Burgoignesc Kandahar-Kandara Trading Company]]'s [[Burgoignesc Kandahar-Kandara Trading Company|Army of the Eastern Expanse]], wrote a treatise for boys about {{wp|reconnaissance}} and {{wp|scouting}}. In the summer of [[1807]] MacArtair-de'Roagueux held a {{wp|Brownsea Island Scout camp|camp}} on [[Ayermer]] to test ideas for his book. This camp and the publication of  ''Scouting for Boys'' are generally regarded as the start of the Scout movement. The organization remained a purely an [[Audonia]]n [[Burgundie|Burgoignesc]] colonial tradition until the returning and fleeing colonists of those places came to [[Crona]], [[Levantia]], and [[Sarpedon]]. Those fleeing with children were seen by the receiving societie"
    },
