      "title": "Crown_Liberalism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Politics_of_Urcea"
      ],
      "snippet": "{{Urcea WIP}}\n\n'''Crown Liberalism''' is a semi-{{wp|liberalism|liberal}} philosophy and political ideology originating in [[Urcea]] based on {{wp|consent of the governed}}, {{wp|Reform movement|gradual reform}}, and the establishment of a government \"guided by but not limited to\" non-elected authorities such as the [[Apostolic King of Urcea]] or [[Catholic Church]]. Crown Liberalism is considered to be {{wp|fusionist}}, embracing core liberal principles such as the {{wp|social contract}} and {{wp|individual rights}} while accepting the necessity of public stability and opposing political {{wp|radicalism}}. It is similar to [[Organicism]] in application inasmuch as Crown Liberalism emphasizes reform and continuity, but major disagreements between Crown Liberalism and Organicism exist on basic issues such as {{wp|social contract}} and class distinctions, as Crown Liberalism is viewed by many to be a definitively {{wp|bourgeoisie}} ideology. The philosophy has been described by the [[Kiravia|Kiravian]] scholar [[P. G. W. Gelema]] \"a movement aimed at creating the maximum degree of liberty and mobility for the individual and the family with the minimum degree of instability\".\n\nIn terms of its philosophical origin, Crown Liberalism has largely been traced back to {{wp|absolutist}} notions in [[Urcea]] regarding the {{wp|social contract}} between the [[Apostolic King of Urcea|Apostolic King]] and [[Urcean people]]. Philosophers and scholars have noted that Crown Liberalism's prima"
    },
    {
      "title": "Devinism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Koré'hetanùa"
      ],
      "snippet": "{{wip}}\n{{OOD}}\n[[File:ADPR flag.png|thumb|250px|Flag of the Austral Democratic People's Republic, the proclaimed center of the Devinist and wider communist world according to the Devinist ideology.]]\nDevinism is a {{wp|List of communist ideologies|communist ideology}} developed by Australic Coscivian {{wp|wp|Post-Marxism|post-Marxist}}, {{wp|economist}}, and {{wp|Military strategy|military strategist}} Grand Admiral Varunor Devin. The ideology proposes the establishment of a {{wp|Market socialism|market socialist}} sphere of states across the world with Australis at its center. Varunor Devin proposed that {{wp|world communism}} could only ultimately be achieved through the forceful military expansion and the gaining of political influence in other countries in order to incite {{wp|world revolution}}. Devin believed communism could only succeed in {{wp|one-party states}} with {{wp|vanguardist}} communist parties dictating national doctrine in each communist state throughout the world. Devin was a firm believer in the concept of the {{wp|nation-state}}, and sought to use the political independence and strong military position of Australis to form a series of communist states around the central [[Koré'hetanùa|Austral Democratic People's Republic]]. This aim was part of the Southern Century initiative undertaken by Devin in the 1910s and is a key component of Devinism to this day. Devin was a staunch opponent of [[Bairdism]], the prevailing communist thought in [[Carna]], as he "
    },
    {
      "title": "Foralism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Castadilla",
        "History_of_Castadilla",
        "Politics_of_Castadilla"
      ],
      "snippet": "{{WIP}}\n'''Foralism''' ([[Castadillaan Pelaxian|Pelaxian]]: ''foralismo''), also known as '''rationalised feudalism''', '''semi-feudalism''', and '''Toryism''' in older [[Ænglish language|Ænglish]]-language sources, is the form of {{wp|traditionalist conservatism}} which governed the [[Viceroyalty of Los Rumas]] and the subsequent [[Delepasian Kingdom]] and [[Delepasian Confederation|Confederation]] from the fall of the [[First Pelaxian Republic]] in 1814 until the 1860s though lingering elements of the philosophy legally remained in place until 1888, and culturally until the rise of the ''[[Estado Social (Castadilla)|Estado Social]]'' in the 1920s. Foralists were staunch {{wp|monarchism|monarchists}}, supporters of the legal system of {{wp|common law}}, wished for the preservation of the traditional decentralised political structure of the Viceroyalty, and were greatly opposed to the {{wp|liberalism}} found in [[Delepasianism]].\n\nForalism emerged from the colonial aristocratic and mercantile opposition to the First Pelaxian Republic, though supporters of the philosophy were not necessarily loyal to the [[House of Girojón]] or any particular royal house; they saw the preservation of the traditional rights as espoused in the [[Charter of San Lina]] as the highest priority, and thus believed that the monarch should have little to no political power. The term is derived from the word ''fuero'', or \"forum\", which referred to constitution-like documents granted to a given class or"
    },
    {
      "title": "Guided_democracy",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies"
      ],
      "snippet": "'''Guided democracy''', also called '''directed democracy''' and '''managed democracy''', is a ''de jure'' {{wp|democracy|democratic}} {{wp|government|government}} that nonetheless functions as a ''de facto'' {{wp|Authoritarianism|authoritarian regime}} or, in some cases, as an outright {{wp|Autocracy|autocratic regime}}. Such forms of {{wp|Hybrid regime|hybrid regimes}} are legitimised by elections, but these elections do not serve to change the {{wp|State (polity)|state}}'s government in any meaningful way but rather are deemed as {{wp|sham election|shams}} designed to keep up the appearance of a {{wp|liberal democracy}}.\n\nIn a guided democratic system, elections are directly controlled and manipulated by the government; people are permitted to exercise their democratic rights, but the government ensures that voters cannot change public policy. While they ostensibly follow basic democratic principles, they generally are beholden to a variety of authoritarianism to keep democracy \"on a leash\". Under a managed democratic system, the state makes heavy use of {{wp|propaganda techniques}} to {{wp|Manufacturing Consent|manufacture consent}} from the governed, and at times might make extensive use of {{wp|electoral fraud}} techniques such as voter intimidation, disinformation, or even announcing a predetermined result without bothering to count the votes, to prevent the electorate from having any kind of impact on government policy.\n\nThis concept is somewhat related to a {{wp|Anoc"
    },
    {
      "title": "Kaidoism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies"
      ],
      "snippet": "[[File:Kaidoist flag.png|330px|thumb|Flag used by Kaidoists between 1840s and 1850s]]\n\n'''Kaidoism''' ([[Pomorskitong]]: ''Kaidoézm'') is a [[Nasastan|Nasastani]] political ideology and movement based on the ideas of Načelnik [[Kréstofer Kaido]] (1800 - 1839). Emerging in the early 19th century, it represents one of the principal ideological offshoots of [[Resistanceism]], often described as Nasastan's own internally developed variant of socialist thought. Kaidoism emphasizes the pursuit of [[Final egalitarianism|final egalitarianism]], the gradual equalization and standardization of all facets of social and economic life under a state-managed order. Although initially associated with Kaido’s rule in the 1830s, the term Kaidoism did not enter common usage until the tenure of Načelnik [[Kats Kontor]] (1844 - 1849), whose public identification with Kaido's legacy led to its formal recognition as a distinct ideological tradition within Nasastani politics.\n\nThe term Kaidoism derives from the surname of Kréstofer Kaido, Načelnik of Nasastan between 1829 and 1839. The suffix -ism (''-ézm'' in Pomorskitong) was first applied during the late 1840s by political commentators under the [[Aristocratic Sovereignty of Nasastan]] to describe Kontor's attempts to restore elements of Kaido’s program.\n\n== Background ==\nAppointed Načelnik of Nasastan in 1829 by King [[Alekšander III Ziółkowski|Alekšander III]], Kaido implemented a series of sweeping reforms that sought to consolidate state powe"
    },
    {
      "title": "Kallistocracy",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Nasastan"
      ],
      "snippet": "[[File:ZD kallistocracy Poster 1937.png|thumb|\"Kallistocracy; Live for Another.\" Recruitment poster for the [[Zabitur'š z duzelzo|ZD Secret Service]], produced in late 1937 to encourage enlistment following the [[Paramilitary Revolution]] and prior to the adoption of the organization's revised insignia.]]\n'''Kallistocracy''' ([[Pomorskitong]]: ''Kallistokracéa'', literally ''government for the best condition'') is a political and philosophical doctrine originating in [[Nasastan]] that holds that the primary purpose of the state is to preserve the most desirable form of ordinary human life for the majority of the population. Rather than defining the state through sovereignty, liberty, or divine authority, Kallistocracy argues that government exists to maximize collective convenience, social stability, and interpersonal trust while minimizing unnecessary struggle. The doctrine constitutes one of the central political expressions of [[Zmoric Philosophy]] and the [[Lakhe'žic]] principle of 'easy life', serving as the dominant ideological framework of the modern Nasastani state.\n\nThe term derives from the [[istroyan]] roots ''kallistos'' (''best'', ''most beautiful'', or ''ideal'') and ''kratos'' (''rule'' or ''government''), collectively interpreted in Nasastani political philosophy as \"government for the best condition.\" Unlike conventional translations such as \"rule by the best,\" the phrase refers not to the excellence of rulers themselves but to the preservation of the best po"
    },
    {
      "title": "Kirosocialism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "KRV"
      ],
      "snippet": "\n\n{{Cquote|The essence of Kirosocialism is a revolutionary theory which has provided solutions to problems arising in the revolutionary praxis in a new age different from the era that gave rise to Marxism–Leninism, one that gives a profound explanation of the theories, strategies and tactics of national liberation, class emancipation and human liberations in our era. Thus, it can be said that Kirosocialism is a creative application of socialist thought to the material - yes - but also social, cultural, and spiritual conditions of Coscivian civilization under capitalism. You see, Marxism–Leninism presented a series of dicta on building socialism with the ultimate goal of world communism, but Marxist–Livasist thought as generally understood cannot escape the confines of presuppositions and hypotheses owing to the limitations of the conditions of their ages and practical experiences. Thus, as many countries applied the principles of Marxist–Leninist materialistic conception of history dogmatically, they have failed to advance revolution continually after the establishment of the socialist system. Kirosocialism, then, is not merely \"Socialism with Coscivian characteristics\". On the contrary, the philosophical and social-scientific synthesis of Kirosocialism has brought us closer to classical Marxism, which expresses the relationship between economics and politics, and further, the economic base and superstructure where the latter is determined fundamentally by the former and only"
    },
