    {
      "title": "Nolanism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Politics_of_Urcea"
      ],
      "snippet": "{{Stub}}\n\n'''Nolanism''' is a strain of {{wp|liberalism|liberal}} thought within [[Urcea]] that advocates the complete adoption of liberal social and economic ideas while remaining neutral on political issues such as the structure of the [[Constitution of Urcea|constitution]] or [[Apostolic King of Urcea|monarchy]] for pragmatic reasons. Nolanists are generally regarded as a radical strain of {{wp|conservative liberalism|conservative liberals}}, with the ideology calling for no accomodation with ancient institutions in Urcea such as the [[Guilds (Urcea)|guild system]], the established [[Catholic Church]], and the prevalence of so-called vice laws. Nolanism is generally opposed to [[Crown Liberalism]] and views the emancipation of the individual and enshrinement of {{wp|civil rights}} at the center of Urcean society as its chief aims. Adherents of the ideology also denounce {{Wp|socialism}} and various left-wing movements, feeling that they often make the monarchy a politically distracting issue while making their civil rights-oriented goals more easily subject to {{wp|strawman}} dismissals. The ideology is named for Linar Orens-Nolan, a onetime [[National Pact (Urcea)|National Pact]] member of the [[Conshilía Daoni]] and mid-20th century thought leader.\n\n[[Category:IXWB]]\n[[Category:Politics of Urcea]]\n[[Category:Political ideologies]]"
    },
    {
      "title": "Organicism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Urcea"
      ],
      "snippet": "{{Urcea WIP}}\n\n'''Organicism''' is a political and moral philosophy based on the existence of organically developed, interconnected societies and public life as an expression of these concepts. Organicists argue that all human persons are social animals and, consequently, humans are created to be governed and live in society. Accordingly, society is not created but is an organic, developing institution based around the organically developed set of social relationships to which all individuals are subject; since this is the case, it is in society's interests to ensure the common good, defined as \"the sum total of social conditions which allow people, either as groups or as individuals, to reach their fulfillment more fully and more easily\".  Organicists espouse a somewhat wide array of views depending on their understanding of these principles, but they generally support {{wp|social democracy}}, organized religion, moral law as public law, degrees of social equality, with some proponents of organicism supporting nationalism or [[organic socialism]].\n\nOrganicism became a distinct movement in the 18th century, when it became popular among [[Occidental]] philosophers and economists as a position contra {{wp|social contract theory}}. Proponents of organicism sought to reform the norms of hereditary privilege, state religion, absolute monarchy, the divine right of kings and traditional conservatism into more inclusive systems without creating a definitive rupture; many viewed it as"
    },
    {
      "title": "Pan-Cronanism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies"
      ],
      "snippet": "'''Pan-Cronanism''', a movement which has recently gained momentum in the 2020's, is the political ideology calling for the unification of states on the continent of [[Crona]] into a larger political entity, commonly in the form of supranational organizations, but also rarely in form of one of several unified Cronan superstates. The movement is most popular in southern Crona, with smaller movements elsewhere on the continent.\n\n==Origins==\nThe Pan-Cronan movement emerged after the end of the [[Great Cronan War]] in the mid-late 1950s. It originally began as a movement of peace, intended to call for peaceful resolution to any further conflicts that could escalate into another Great War. As time went on, however, the movement grew into one that sought to join together the continent of Crona in some respect of unity and cooperation. Kronatan congressman [[Gregor Friedrich Mandel]] coined the term ''Pan-Cronanism'' in his 1961 speech to the [[Kronatan Legislature|Bundeskongress]], saying that \"It is a necessary idea of brotherly cooperation between all the states of Crona to rebuild the relationships shaken by the war.\"\n\n[[Category:IXWB]]\n[[Category:Political ideologies]]"
    },
    {
      "title": "Pelaxian_Socialism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Pelaxia"
      ],
      "snippet": "Pelaxian Socialism is a political, social, and economic philosophy of Marxist origing encompassing a range political theories, and social systems characterised by social ownership of the means of production. It includes the economic policies and movements associated with such systems. Social ownership can be public, collective, cooperative, or of equity. \n\nWhile no single definition encapsulates the many types of principles, social ownership is the one common element that has been higlighted in the 21st century. \n\nHistorically Pelaxian Socialists disagreed about the degree to which social control or regulation of the economy is necessary, how far society should intervene and whether government, particularly existing government, is the correct vehicle for change. Pelaxian Socialism lines of thought have been divided in non-market and market forms. Non-market socialism substitutes factor markets and money with integrated economic planning and engineering or technical criteria based on calculation performed in-kind, thereby producing a different economic mechanism that functions according to different economic laws and dynamics than those of capitalism. A non-market socialist system eliminates the inefficiencies and crises traditionally associated with capital accumulation and the profit system in capitalism. The socialist calculation debate, originated by the economic calculation problem, concerns the feasibility and methods of resource allocation for a planned socialist system"
    },
    {
      "title": "Political_ideologies_in_Castadilla",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Castadilla",
        "Politics_of_Castadilla"
      ],
      "snippet": "Formal '''political ideologies in Castadilla''' are aligned based upon the {{wp|left-right political spectrum}}, an axis used to classify political positions in relation to their emphasis on either social equality or social hierarchy. Most [[Castadillaans]] identify as at least one of four main ideologies: {{wp|socialism}}, {{wp|liberalism}}, {{wp|conservatism}}, and {{wp|nationalism}}; it is very common for one to identify as being any combination of two of the four main ideologies, with {{wp|political polarization|political polarisation}} being largely miniscule for the most part thanks to the four ideological groups more or less cooperating with each other during the era of the provisional government after the end of the [[Velvet Revolution]].\n\n[[Castadilla]], alongside [[Pelaxia]], have often been called the \"cradle of socialism\", and the main subsections within modern [[Socialism in Castadilla|Castadillaan socialism]] include [[Velvetine Socialism]], [[Livasism#Borbonism|Borbonism]], [[Livasism#Cabaceroism|Cabaceroism]], and even various flavours of socialist-aligned {{wp|social democracy}}; with Velvetine Socialism and Borbonism having emerged from the revolutionary left-wing factions of the Velvet Revolution while socialist-aligned social democracy developed throughout the latter half of the 19th Century and had gained prominence as the left-wing faction of the anti-Pascualist opposition groups which were formed in the late 1940s. Cabaceroism emerged in the 1930s as an"
    },
    {
      "title": "Puertegan_Path_to_Socialism",
      "categories": [
        "Political_ideologies",
        "Puertego"
      ],
      "snippet": "Puertegan Path to Socialism {{lang|es|(camino al socialismo de Puertego)}} is a {{wp|Marxist revisionist}} {{wp|socialist}} ideology based around a set of political theories and policies advocated for by the dominant party of Puertego the Puertegan White Flag communist Party that are seen by their proponents as representing {{wp|Marxism–Leninism}} but adapted and reformed to best serve Puertego for the modern circumstances it finds itself in. Most people consider the largest modern proponent of this ideology to be the current president of Puertego Matthias Torres, the primary principles advocated by him for the ideology are an opening of the formally state controlled economy to the rest of the world, a strong central government to help oversee this transition and to maintain public order during this transition, and the protecting of Puertegan culture and traditions in society.\n\n== History ==\nThe name of the ideology was first coined by Erik Alférez the founder of the Puertegan White Flag Communist Party and first president of Puertego who called it \"the only way I can see Puertego having a future on the world stage\". Since that time however the ideology has branched out massively from Alférez's original vision which was focused largely on social and political reforms and only had minor plans for economic reforms to prevent the economy from collapsing however Alférez did not live long enough to enshrine this aspect of the ideology into law as he died from a stroke after only 3"
    },
